# local env files
# `.env` alone matches only that exact filename, and `.env*.local` matches
# `.env.production.local` but not `.env.production` — which Expo CLI also loads.
# `.env.*` covers every per-environment variant; the negation keeps the
# committed template.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# generated native folders
/ios
/android

# local dev QR code
expo-qr.png

# Pre-optimisation exam artwork.
#
# `assets/exam/` ships JPEG re-encodes capped at 1000px on the long edge, which
# is the widest the artwork viewer can ever display (see `ArtworkViewer`) and
# 78% smaller than the PNG scans they came from -- 48.4 MB to 10.8 MB. The
# originals are kept here so the encode can be redone at a different quality
# without re-scanning, and ignored because nothing imports them: Metro bundles
# what is `require`d, so an unreferenced directory costs nothing in the app but
# would cost 48 MB in the repo.
assets/exam-source/

# Supabase CLI local state (project metadata, regenerated by `supabase link`)
supabase/.temp/

# Store submission credentials.
#
# `eas.json` points `submit.production.android.serviceAccountKeyPath` here. That
# JSON is a Google Cloud service-account private key with upload rights to the
# Play listing -- committing it hands anyone with repo access the ability to
# publish releases. The `*.p8`/`*.p12` rules above already cover the Apple side.
# `secrets/*` rather than `secrets/`, because git cannot re-include a file whose
# parent *directory* is excluded — the negation below is silently dead against a
# trailing-slash rule. Excluding the contents keeps the instructions for
# obtaining the key next to where the key goes. Same pattern as `!.env.example`.
secrets/*
!secrets/README.md

# Written by `src/content/dump-content.test.ts` for the coverage sweep.
audit-dump.json
